### 4. COMPLETE USER WORKFLOW

Implement the following end-to-end workflow.

STEP 1 — User registration and role selection

Provide separate experiences for:
- Collector
- Shop owner or collection-centre manager
- Recycler
- Administrator

For the prototype, provide secure authentication or a clearly labelled development/demo login. Users must not be able to access another user's private inventory or privileged administration functions.

STEP 2 — Collector dashboard

The collector should see:
- Scan electronic waste
- Today's scanned items
- Current crate inventory
- Approximate accumulated quantities
- Items requiring review
- Hazard alerts
- Eligible pickup requests
- Marketplace activity
- Completed pickups
- Estimated and confirmed earnings
- Language settings

Keep the home screen simple and mobile-friendly.

STEP 3 — Snap-to-Sort scanning

The collector opens the camera or uploads a photograph.

The application processes the image and returns:
- Predicted category
- Confidence score
- Item or component description, when supported
- Appropriate crate colour
- A large visual category icon
- Simple sorting instructions
- Safety warnings
- Whether human confirmation is required
- Whether the result is safe to accept automatically

Provide two modes:

A. Whole-device mode:
Identify a whole device such as a phone, television, charger, computer, or fan. Show a cautious, high-level guide to the likely material categories present.

B. Separated-component mode:
Classify a component that has already been separated, such as a circuit board, cable, motor, battery, display, or plastic casing.

Do not instruct users to open dangerous devices, cut cables, dismantle batteries, handle hazardous internal components, or perform unsafe extraction. Direct hazardous or uncertain dismantling situations to trained professionals.

STEP 4 — Safety decision

Before suggesting a crate, evaluate the predicted category, confidence, and hazard-related rules.

If the result is uncertain, display a prominent “Needs Review” state.

If the system detects a possible battery or hazardous item, display the appropriate warning and conservative handling guidance.

A dangerous item must never be classified as safe merely because the model gives a low-risk category a high score.

STEP 5 — Confirm or correct

Let the collector:
- Confirm the proposed category.
- Correct an incorrect category.
- Send the item for review.
- Cancel the scan.

Record corrections for future dataset improvement, but do not automatically treat every user correction as verified ground truth.

STEP 6 — Crate inventory

After confirmation, record the item in the appropriate crate inventory.

Track:
- Item ID
- Collector ID
- Category
- Predicted class
- Confirmed class
- Confidence score
- Safety flags
- Scan time
- Confirmation status
- Crate ID
- Quantity or weight, when measured
- Weight measurement source
- Correction history

Do not invent a physical weight from a photograph. If weight is unavailable, request a manual entry or use a clearly labelled demo value.

STEP 7 — Pickup eligibility

Each crate or material lot has a configurable pickup threshold.

When its verified accumulated quantity reaches that threshold, offer the collector an option to create a pickup request.

A threshold can be based on weight, quantity, or a configurable rule, depending on the material and recycler requirements.

Do not assume that every category has the same threshold.

STEP 8 — Recycler marketplace

Eligible recyclers can view compatible material lots and submit bids.

The collector can compare:
- Offered price per kilogram or other explicitly defined unit
- Expected total value
- Material category
- Reported quantity or weight
- Pickup location at an appropriate level of detail
- Proposed pickup time
- Recycler verification status
- Bid expiry
- Purity and acceptance conditions

The marketplace should support bid submission, editing before expiry, acceptance, rejection, withdrawal when allowed, and bid status tracking.

Only eligible recyclers should see and bid on relevant categories.

STEP 9 — Pickup and purity verification

After accepting a bid:
- Create a pickup record.
- Assign a pickup status.
- Record the planned date and time.
- Allow the collector to mark the material ready.
- Allow the recycler to confirm collection.
- Record actual measured weight.
- Record the purity or acceptance result.
- Record any quantity or price adjustment.
- Store evidence or notes when available.

Possible statuses:

Draft → Open for Bids → Bid Accepted → Pickup Scheduled → Collected → Quality Verified → Payment Recorded → Completed.

Provide cancellation and dispute states where appropriate.

Do not allow users to mark a pickup as completed without the required workflow checks.

STEP 10 — Payment demonstration

The academic prototype must use simulated payments unless a real payment provider has been deliberately integrated and tested.

Provide:
- Agreed amount
- Final verified amount
- Simulated payment reference
- Payment status
- Transaction history
- Timestamp
- Clear “Demo Payment — No Money Transferred” wording

Do not collect real UPI credentials, banking passwords, or payment PINs.

